3. Data keeps its own license
- Australian Shark-Incident Database: CC BY 4.0. Cite: Meagher, P. (2026). Australian Shark Incident Database. Zenodo. https://zenodo.org/records/18752301 — and Riley et al. (2022), Scientific Data, for the database description.
- HadISST sea surface temperature: © British Crown Copyright, Met Office, provided under the Open Government Licence. Cite Rayner et al. (2003), J. Geophys. Res. 108(D14), 4407, doi:10.1029/2002JD002670; acknowledge www.metoffice.gov.uk/hadobs.
- CHIRPS rainfall: public domain (CC0). Cite Funk et al. (2015), The climate hazards infrared precipitation with stations, Scientific Data 2, 150066, doi:10.1038/sdata.2015.66.
- NOAA GHCN rain gauge records: public domain (US Government work). Cite Menne, M.J. et al. (2012), Global Historical Climatology Network - Daily (GHCN-Daily), NOAA National Climatic Data Center, doi:10.7289/V5D21VHZ.
- River mouths: © OpenStreetMap contributors, Open Database License (ODbL) 1.0, https://www.openstreetmap.org/copyright. Coastline: Natural Earth, public domain.
- Réunion and New Caledonia shark-bite records: CC BY 4.0. Cite Taglioni, F., Guiltat, S. and Delsaut, M., Datasets of Human-shark interactions in New Caledonia and Reunion Island (1980-2022), Zenodo, doi:10.5281/zenodo.12549370. A copy is kept as src/islands_nc_run.csv.
- California shark incidents: California Department of Fish and Wildlife, Shark Incident Database, CC BY, https://lab.data.ca.gov/dataset/shark-incident-database-california.
- Fremantle sea level: Permanent Service for Mean Sea Level (PSMSL). Cite Holgate, S.J. et al. (2013), New Data Systems and Products at the Permanent Service for Mean Sea Level, Journal of Coastal Research 29(3), 493-504.
- Fremantle sea level, recent months: University of Hawaii Sea Level Center, station 175, fast-delivery daily means, free to use and redistribute. Cite Caldwell, P.C., Merrifield, M.A. and Thompson, P.R. (2015), Sea level measured by tide gauges from global oceans, the Joint Archive for Sea Level holdings (NCEI Accession 0019568), NOAA NCEI, doi:10.7289/V5V40S7W.
- IMOS Animal Tracking receiver installations: CC BY 4.0. Cite IMOS, IMOS - Animal Tracking Facility - Receiver locations, https://geoserver-123.aodn.org.au/geoserver/ows.
- CMIP6 climate model output: licensed by each modelling centre (mostly CC BY 4.0); cite the World Climate Research Programme CMIP6 and each model used (listed in cmip_tos_delta_ssp245.csv on the ocean-data branch).
- GBIF occurrence counts: GBIF.org; each source dataset keeps its own license (CC0, CC BY or CC BY-NC).
- NASA MODIS Aqua Kd490 water clarity: NASA open data, no restrictions on use. Acknowledge NASA Ocean Biology Processing Group.
- IMOS Animal Tracking acoustic detections: CC BY 4.0. Cite: IMOS Animal Tracking Facility, Integrated Marine Observing System (IMOS). IMOS is enabled by the National Collaborative Research Infrastructure Strategy (NCRIS).
- Queensland Shark Control Program data (catches, gear locations): Queensland Government open data, CC BY 4.0. Credit: Queensland Department of Primary Industries, Shark Control Program.
- ERSSTv5 sea surface temperature: NOAA NCEI, public domain (US Government work). Cite Huang, B. et al. (2017), Extended Reconstructed Sea Surface Temperature, Version 5 (ERSSTv5), J. Climate 30, 8179-8205, doi:10.1175/JCLI-D-16-0836.1.
- NOAA OISST v2.1 daily sea surface temperature: NOAA NCEI, public domain (US Government work). Cite Huang, B. et al. (2021), J. Climate 34, 2923-2939. Marine heatwaves follow Hobday, A.J. et al. (2016), Progress in Oceanography 141, 227-238.
- Today's sea temperature at each beach: Open-Meteo Marine Weather API, CC BY 4.0, https://open-meteo.com/. Used on its free, non-commercial terms.
- Indian Ocean Dipole (Dipole Mode Index from HadISST): NOAA Physical Sciences Laboratory, public domain. Cite Saji, N.H. et al. (1999), Nature 401, 360-363.
- Shark occurrence records: Ocean Biodiversity Information System (OBIS), https://obis.org. Each dataset keeps its own license (mostly CC0 or CC BY; a few CC BY-NC, which may not be used commercially). Cite OBIS (2026), Ocean Biodiversity Information System, Intergovernmental Oceanographic Commission of UNESCO, and each dataset used.
- Ponta do Ouro bull and tiger shark tracking: Ocean Tracking Network and the South African Institute for Aquatic Biodiversity (R. Daly), via OBIS, CC BY 4.0, https://obis.org/dataset/a8c9f694-bb07-451d-9ac0-c8d6b3119c1b.
- Coastal population: GHS-POP R2023A, European Commission Joint Research Centre, CC BY 4.0. Cite Schiavina, M. et al. (2023), doi:10.2905/2FF68A52-5B5B-4A22-8F40-C41DA8332CFE.
- NSW beach water quality: Beachwatch and Harbourwatch enterococci results, NSW Government, CC BY, https://www.beachwatch.nsw.gov.au/ (via the TidyTuesday 2025-05-20 cleaned copy, https://github.com/rfordatascience/tidytuesday).
- Coastal drownings: Surf Life Saving Australia, National Coastal Safety Report 2025 (figures cited, © Surf Life Saving Australia). Population by decade: Australian Bureau of Statistics (figures cited).
- Figures quoted from published studies remain the work of their authors; see the page's source list.

4. Fonts and illustrations
- The fonts in fonts/ (Bricolage Grotesque, Figtree, Martian Mono) are under the SIL Open Font License 1.1; see fonts/OFL.txt. They are not covered by the MIT License.
- The tiger and bull shark outlines are traced from public-domain drawings (Robbie Cada; NOAA) via Wikimedia Commons, credited on the page. The white shark and the other outlines were drawn by the author from field-guide anatomy.

